<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Has anyone gotten to "play" with the SC32?
<C.TING> Jax.  I have touched one and used it slightly.  mighty fast.
<[Gary] GARY-S> Has the SC32 added any enhancements or is it pretty much the 
J-H FRISC3
<C.TING> Dr. S. Y. Tang got SFSU to buy one SC32 for him...
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Did he build board or buy bus card?
<[Gary] GARY-S> Do either of you know ?
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> I only have docs for SC32 ... we were thinking of building 
SBC based on SC32.
<C.TING> It is SC32 card from Silicon Composers.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Nice card.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Forth on it ok?
<C.TING> The Forth is 83-Standard.  Very comfortable....
<C.TING> Dr. Tang is developing multiple precesion math Package for it...
<C.TING> He may use it to do decryption experiments.
<C.TING> I am implementing the same on RTX.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> I wanted to build a desktop computer based on four forth 
engines of some type in parallel.
<C.TING> Jax.  That's exactly what I am doing now.  I got a project from Harris 
to build....
<C.TING> a four RTX system to speed up my very fast Continuous Fourier 
Transform algorith...
<C.TING> to analyze real time signal to 1KHz.
<C.TING> However, it will not be a general purpose workstation.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> That's interesting ... it seems that as the era of Parallel 
Computing emerges, the makers of specialty chips will have to rethink
their business ... DSP perhaps could be handled more congenially in parallel 
than in a custom chip.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Are you still VAXing these days?
<C.TING> The hard part is the interprocessor communication.
<C.TING> No VAX anymore! Not even if disguised as microVAX.
<[Gary] GARY-S> HOW do you allocate jobs ?
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> How are you handling the interproc communications?
<C.TING> I wanted to build a system along the SIMD line...
<C.TING> Single-Instruction-Multiple-Datapath...
<C.TING> But it is very difficult.  For the time being, I will broadcast input 
to all ....
<C.TING> Processors and get results back on a data bus.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Have you seen the many implementations of Transputer (c) 
systems?
<C.TING> I cannot afford the transputers.  Serial communication seems the most 
logic...
<C.TING> way to connect high power processors.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> The most interesting feature ...
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> is that several implementations of multi-xputer systems ...
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> allow dynamic reconfiguration of the grid via software! 
Suddenly ...
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> under program control, your SIMD can become a MIMD, a 
pipeline, whatever.
<[Gary] GARY-S> After many revisions the CUBE still loses data
<C.TING> I do not have much affinity to MIMD structures.  SIMD is much easier 
to...
<C.TING> think and to program, while very powerful for specific problems.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> On the other hand, I confess a taste for PARLOG-type setups.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> which is *quite* MIMD.
<[Gary] GARY-S> elaborate please
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Parallel Prolog is being mapped to many hardware setups 
these days.
<[Gary] GARY-S> tnx
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Hardware prolog processors are in the pipeline for 
foundrying.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> Very interesting field.
<[Gary] GARY-S> just proves Boole really did have a good idea
<C.TING> I am thinking of a structure to solve matrix problems.  Many identical 
processors...
<C.TING> are linked to gether to process columns and rows of data...
<[Gary] GARY-S> APL seems to do pretty darn good with Matrices
<C.TING> This is clearly a SIMD problem.
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> APL, Gary, offers nice *syntax* for matrix problems ...
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> solution is still computationally cpu-consuming!
<[Jax] FIGCHAPTERS> I hear that NASA is reprogramming the MPP in C! Arrgh!
<[Gary] GARY-S> It does do a nice job - if you can find zeta keyboards
